WEBVTT

NOTE Don't Paste Secrets — captions generated from the narration script

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.490
The error belongs in the chat.

2
00:00:02.490 --> 00:00:05.210
The password sitting next to it does not.

3
00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:10.883
Say a deploy is failing. You copy the error, and the config, so the bot can see everything.

4
00:00:10.883 --> 00:00:15.388
Mid-paste you catch it: a production database password, sitting in the block.

5
00:00:15.388 --> 00:00:18.547
You stop, replace it with REDACTED, and send the rest.

6
00:00:18.547 --> 00:00:21.530
The bot can still help. The password never went in.

7
00:00:21.880 --> 00:00:27.362
A chat is closer to emailing a contractor than to writing on a whiteboard in a locked room.

8
00:00:27.362 --> 00:00:32.060
Once a secret is in the thread, asking the bot to forget it does not unsay it.

9
00:00:32.410 --> 00:00:34.264
This shows up in public reports.

10
00:00:34.264 --> 00:00:37.856
Production database credentials pasted into a chatbot at work.

11
00:00:37.856 --> 00:00:41.216
An API key pasted into a chat, and noticed only afterward.

12
00:00:41.216 --> 00:00:45.330
And people asking whether the keys they handed over now need replacing.

13
00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:51.590
Here's the tell. If that line would be a problem on a postcard, it doesn't go in the chat.

14
00:00:51.940 --> 00:01:01.970
Before you paste, scan. Keys, tokens, passwords, connection strings, session cookies, customer personal data, internal URLs that aren't public.

15
00:01:01.970 --> 00:01:04.144
Replace them with placeholders.

16
00:01:04.144 --> 00:01:09.544
If a secret did go in, treat the paste as a leak, and change that credential.

17
00:01:09.544 --> 00:01:12.490
Asking the chat to forget it is not a fix.

18
00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:16.671
Scan before you paste. Replace secrets with placeholders.

19
00:01:16.671 --> 00:01:19.830
If one slipped through, change that credential.

20
00:01:19.830 --> 00:01:21.510
The chat cannot unsay it.

21
00:01:21.860 --> 00:01:25.241
Your turn. Think of the last thing you pasted into a chat.

22
00:01:25.241 --> 00:01:28.680
Reply with the one kind of secret you'd now scan for first.

23
00:01:29.030 --> 00:01:32.710
Show the problem. Keep the secrets out.
